WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:17.173
Idea. Pathogens are microorganisms that cause disease, such as bacteria and viruses. The body defends itself in two lines. The first line is barriers that keep pathogens out. If pathogens get past them, white blood cells are the second line.

00:00:17.750 --> 00:00:30.057
Physical barriers stop pathogens getting in. The skin is a tough outer layer. In the airways, sticky mucus traps pathogens, and tiny hairs called cilia sweep the mucus up to the throat.

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:47.219
Method. Chemical barriers kill pathogens or slow their growth. Acid in the stomach kills pathogens in food. Tears contain enzymes that kill bacteria. Enzymes are proteins that speed up reactions, such as breaking things down.

00:00:47.800 --> 00:00:58.058
Now the second line. A phagocyte is a white blood cell that engulfs pathogens. It flows around the pathogen. This process is called phagocytosis.

00:00:58.650 --> 00:01:06.137
The pathogen is now enclosed in a vacuole inside the cell. A vacuole is a small sac surrounded by membrane.

00:01:06.700 --> 00:01:17.151
Lysosomes are small sacs of digestive enzymes. A lysosome joins with the vacuole. Its enzymes digest the pathogen into small pieces.

00:01:17.750 --> 00:01:27.442
Finally, the left-over pieces are removed from the cell. Phagocytes attack any pathogen, not one particular type, so we call them non-specific.

00:01:28.750 --> 00:01:42.460
Lymphocytes are a second kind of white blood cell. They do not engulf. They make antibodies, which stick to pathogens. Some make antitoxins, which neutralise toxins: the poisons that pathogens release.

00:01:43.050 --> 00:01:58.163
Some people catch infections more easily. Elderly people often have a weaker immune system: the body's defence against pathogens. And crowded indoor spaces with poor air flow let pathogens spread from person to person.

00:01:59.300 --> 00:02:18.259
Worked example. Now a real exam question. The diagram shows a white blood cell engulfing a bacterium, and three stages that follow. A lysosome is labelled: it contains digestive enzymes. Describe what is happening at stages 1, 2 and 3. Three marks.

00:02:18.850 --> 00:02:28.279
Stage 1 is the arrow from the lysosome to the vacuole holding the bacterium. So at stage 1, the lysosome joins with the vacuole.

00:02:28.850 --> 00:02:35.717
Stage 2 shows the bacterium in small pieces. The enzymes from the lysosome are digesting it.

00:02:36.300 --> 00:02:44.255
Stage 3 shows a sac of small pieces leaving the cell. The remains of the bacterium are removed from the white blood cell.

00:02:44.850 --> 00:02:52.711
Here is the full answer, one sentence per stage. This answer is by olevels.com, not an official mark scheme.

00:02:53.850 --> 00:03:05.635
Common mistake. A common mistake is to say the phagocyte eats the bacterium. That earns nothing. Say it engulfs the bacterium, and enzymes from lysosomes digest it.

00:03:06.200 --> 00:03:18.283
Another mistake is to mix up the jobs. Antibodies do not digest, and lymphocytes do not engulf. Phagocytes engulf, enzymes digest, and lymphocytes make antibodies.

00:03:19.400 --> 00:03:31.686
Your turn. A girl cuts her finger, and bacteria get into the cut. Name the barrier that has been broken. Then describe how a phagocyte destroys the bacteria. Pause here and work it out.

00:03:32.800 --> 00:03:37.859
Answer. Part a: the skin. The cut breaks it, so the bacteria can get in.

00:03:38.450 --> 00:03:50.469
Part b: the phagocyte engulfs the bacteria into a vacuole. Lysosomes join the vacuole. Their enzymes digest the bacteria. Then the remains are removed from the cell.
